  "summary": "The beaded collar like a falling waterfall — Greenland's most radiant garment.",
  "description": "The West Greenlandic national costume (kalaallisuut) grew out of 18th-century colonial-era clothing — tricolor kollaat skirts, silk shawls, kamik boots — reworked into a spectacular festival dress. Its famous feature is the nunat: a huge beaded collar-wingspan on the woman's kalaallisuut, thousands of glass beads worked in geometric falls of red, blue, white and yellow from shoulders to elbows like a frozen waterfall. Men wear white anorak-style attakkattat with black boots. Worn at confirmations, weddings and Greenland's National Day (21 June), the costume is hand-measured and bead-collared by family masters, each nunat pattern a family inheritance.",
  "culturalContext": "The kalaallisuut is Greenland's identity garment: colonial trade cloth reworked into national festival dress, the beaded nunat collar a family inheritance, National Day (21 June) and confirmations its stage. Costume ateliers sustain beadwork transmission, and the dress carries Kalaallit self-assertion within the Danish realm.",
